---
description: CLI-driven software factory.
---

***definitions***

- :Project: identifies a repository registered with the factory, described by [resources/project.schema.json](resources/project.schema.json).

- :Task: is a unit of work requested for one repository.

- :TaskFile: is the authoritative JSON record of a :Task:, described by [resources/task-record.schema.json](resources/task-record.schema.json).

- :IntakeId: identifies a :Task: from submission.

- :RepoProfile: records the approved settings and safety limits for work in a repository.

- :StepDefinition: names a bounded model step, its prompt, input and output names, and model ladder.

- :ModelLadder: is an ordered sequence of Claude model and reasoning-effort rungs.

- :LoopDefinition: is a named, ordered path of steps and gates for a :Task:.

- :ClassifierVerdict: records the classifier's facts about a :Task:, described by [resources/classifier-verdict.schema.json](resources/classifier-verdict.schema.json).

- :ReadinessDecision: describes the next readiness action for a :Task:, in [resources/readiness-decision.schema.json](resources/readiness-decision.schema.json).

- :ClassifierRequest: contains the :TaskFile:, classifier prompt and first model rung, described by [resources/classifier-request.schema.json](resources/classifier-request.schema.json).

- :EngineHealthApi: is the local engine health contract in [resources/engine-health.openapi.yaml](resources/engine-health.openapi.yaml).

- :ReviewOutput: records the reviewer's verdict, summary and findings, described by [resources/review.schema.json](resources/review.schema.json).

- :Gate: is a check the driver enforces before a :Task: may move on; the human approval gate binds an approval to one reviewed commit.

***implementation reqs***

- :Implementation: uses pnpm workspaces, Node.js 26 or later, ESM and strict TypeScript limited to erasable syntax, run directly by Node without a build step. `bin/factory` is the executable.

- The domain core is pure and has no I/O; the driver coordinates adapters through interfaces and uses plain async functions with typed error classes (see docs/adr/0002-hand-written-v1.md; Effect is deferred).

- The model-runner adapter uses the Claude Agent SDK with the machine's own Claude sign-in or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`. It takes model and effort from the request or ladder rung, loads no user or repository Claude settings, and routes every tool call through the step's guard.

- Default step, ladder and loop definitions and step prompts live under `definitions/`.

- :UnitTests: of model execution use a scripted fake of the model-runner interface; they never call real model APIs, GitHub or git remotes.

- The :Task: domain is pure and resides in `packages/core`; filesystem, git, GitHub, process and Claude access reside in `packages/adapters-*`, workflow coordination in `packages/driver`, and `apps/cli` composes them.
- :UnitTests: use test-driven development: write a failing test before the implementation that makes it pass.

- :RepoProfile:, :StepDefinition: and :ModelLadder: YAML are validated with zod.

- :LoopDefinition: YAML is validated with zod.

***functional specs***

# cli-help
Running `factory --help` prints `Usage: factory [options]` and exits with status 0.

# submit-task
`factory intake --project <project> --repo <absolute-path> <prompt>` creates a :TaskFile: and prints its :IntakeId: followed by a newline, then exits 0.
- The data root is `FACTORY_ROOT` when set, otherwise `~/.local/share/factory`; the :TaskFile: is `<data-root>/<project>/tasks/<id>/task.json`.
- The :IntakeId: is a newly generated UUID. The :TaskFile: contains `id`, `project`, `repo`, `prompt`, `stage`, `created_at`, `updated_at` and an `events` timeline, with `stage` set to `queued`; `prompt` is preserved verbatim.
- `project` is a nonempty name containing only ASCII letters, digits, hyphens and underscores; `repo` is an existing absolute directory; `prompt` is nonempty after trimming. Invalid input exits nonzero without creating a :Task:.
- `--repo` may be omitted when the project is registered; the registered :Project: repository is used.

# show-task
`factory task show --project <project> --id <id>` reads the :TaskFile: at `<data-root>/<project>/tasks/<id>/task.json`, prints it as one JSON value followed by a newline, and exits 0.
- The data root and project name follow `submit-task`; `id` is a UUID. The record conforms to :TaskFile: and its `project` and `id` match the arguments.
- Invalid arguments or a missing, malformed or mismatched :TaskFile: exit nonzero with an error on standard error and leave task files unchanged.

# list-tasks
`factory task list --project <project>` prints the project's :TaskFile: records as a JSON array followed by a newline and exits 0.
- The data root and project name follow `submit-task`. Records are read from `task.json` in each direct child task directory under `<data-root>/<project>/tasks/`, validated against :TaskFile:, and sorted lexicographically by `id`.
- An absent tasks directory produces `[]`. An invalid project or an unreadable, malformed, or project- or directory-id-mismatched record exits nonzero with an error on standard error and prints no partial array. No task files are changed.

# check-profile
`factory profile check --project <project>` validates the :RepoProfile: at `<data-root>/<project>/profile.yaml` and prints `Profile valid: <project>` followed by a newline, then exits 0.
- The data root and project name follow `submit-task`.
- The YAML mapping contains `project` equal to the argument, nonempty strings `approved_at`, `repo.base_branch.value`, `repo.base_branch.source`, `commands.test` and `commands.full_check`, and string lists `confirmed.forbidden_paths`, `confirmed.forbidden_commands`, `design_sources` and `qa_targets`; the lists may be empty.
- A missing, malformed, unapproved or invalid :RepoProfile: exits nonzero with an error identifying the problem and changes no :TaskFile:.

# check-step-definition
`factory definition check-step <path>` validates a :StepDefinition: YAML file and its prompt, prints `Step valid: <name>` followed by a newline, and exits 0.
- The YAML mapping contains nonempty strings `name`, `prompt` and `model`, and lists of nonempty strings `inputs` and `outputs`; either list may be empty. `model` names a model ladder.
- `prompt` is a relative path to an existing file, resolved from the YAML file's directory.
- A missing or invalid definition or prompt exits nonzero with an error identifying the problem. No model is called.

# check-model-ladder
`factory definition check-ladder <path>` validates a :ModelLadder: YAML file, prints `Ladder valid: <name>` followed by a newline, and exits 0.
- The YAML mapping contains a nonempty `name` and a nonempty `rungs` list. Each rung has a unique nonempty `name`, a `model` string starting with `claude-`, and an `effort` of `low`, `medium`, `high` or `max`.
- A missing or invalid :ModelLadder: exits nonzero with an error identifying the problem. No model is called.

# check-loop-definition
`factory definition check-loop <path>` validates a :LoopDefinition: YAML file, prints `Loop valid: <name>` followed by a newline, and exits 0.
- The YAML mapping contains a nonempty string `name`, a boolean `produces_code` and a nonempty `entries` list. Each entry has a `kind` of `step` or `gate` and a nonempty string `name`.
- When `produces_code` is true, the entries include, in order, step `coder`, gate `verify`, step `reviewer`, gate `human-review`, gate `decisions-clear` and gate `open-pr`. Other entries may appear between them.
- A missing or invalid :LoopDefinition: exits nonzero with an error identifying the problem. No model is called.

# check-classifier-verdict
`factory verdict check <path>` validates a JSON :ClassifierVerdict:, prints `Verdict valid: <intake_id>` followed by a newline, and exits 0.
- An empty `blockers` list requires `readiness` to be `ready`. Otherwise, `readiness` names the first blocker kind present in the order research, clarification, slicing, with the `needs-` prefix.
- A missing, malformed or inconsistent :ClassifierVerdict: exits nonzero with an error on standard error and prints nothing on standard output. No model is called and no route is selected.

# record-classifier-verdict
`factory task record-verdict --project <project> --id <id> --verdict <path>` records a :ClassifierVerdict: in an existing :TaskFile: and prints `Verdict recorded: <id>` followed by a newline, then exits 0.
- The task is read and validated as in `show-task`; the verdict is validated as in `check-classifier-verdict`, and its `intake_id` must equal the task's `id`.
- The `verdict` property is atomically replaced with the validated verdict and a `verdict` event is appended; all other task properties, including `stage`, are preserved.
- Invalid or mismatched arguments, task or verdict, or a failed write exits nonzero with an error on standard error, prints nothing on standard output, and leaves the :TaskFile: unchanged. No model is called and no route is selected.

# task-readiness
`factory task readiness --project <project> --id <id>` reads the task as in `show-task` and prints its :ReadinessDecision: as a JSON object followed by a newline, then exits 0.
- The task must have a :ClassifierVerdict: valid as in `check-classifier-verdict`, whose `intake_id` equals the task's `id`.
- An `unsupported` kind produces action `unsupported`; otherwise readiness `ready`, `needs-research`, `needs-clarification` and `needs-slicing` produce actions `continue`, `research`, `ask-human` and `slice` respectively.
- Invalid or missing task or verdict exits nonzero with an error on standard error and prints nothing on standard output. The command changes no task, calls no model, selects no loop and executes no work.

# prepare-task-worktree
`factory task prepare-worktree --project <project> --id <id>` creates a dedicated git worktree and prints its absolute path followed by a newline, then exits 0.
- The task, project profile and readiness are validated as in `show-task`, `check-profile` and `task-readiness`; the readiness action must be `continue`. The task repository must be a git repository with a clean working tree and an existing approved base branch.
- The worktree is `<data-root>/<project>/worktrees/<id>`, on a new branch `factory/<id>` created from `repo.base_branch.value` in the approved profile.
- On success the :TaskFile: records `worktree` with its path, branch and base branch.
- Invalid input, an existing worktree path or branch, or a git failure exits nonzero with an error on standard error and no standard output. A failed creation removes any worktree and branch created by this command; it never changes an existing branch, worktree or :TaskFile:.

# verify-task-worktree
`factory task verify --project <project> --id <id>` runs the approved profile's `commands.test` and then `commands.full_check` in the task's dedicated worktree.
- The task and profile are validated as in `show-task` and `check-profile`. The worktree at `<data-root>/<project>/worktrees/<id>` must belong to the task's repository and be on branch `factory/<id>`; the command never falls back to the original repository.
- Each command runs with `/bin/sh -c`, with its output forwarded to standard error. A nonzero exit stops verification before the next command.
- Verification fails when the commands leave uncommitted or untracked, non-ignored files in the worktree.
- If both commands succeed, the command prints `Verification passed: <id>` followed by a newline and exits 0. Invalid input, a missing or mismatched worktree, or a failed command exits nonzero with an error on standard error and no standard output. Verification does not update the :TaskFile: or commit changes.

# prepare-classifier-run
`factory task prepare-classifier --project <project> --id <id> --step <path> --ladder <path>` prints a :ClassifierRequest: as one JSON object followed by a newline and exits 0.
- The task, approved project profile, step and ladder are validated as in `show-task`, `check-profile`, `check-step-definition` and `check-model-ladder`. The step's `name` is `classifier` and its `model` matches the ladder's `name`.
- The request contains the complete task, the UTF-8 contents of the step's prompt file, and the `model` and `effort` of the first ladder rung.
- Invalid or unreadable input exits nonzero with an error on standard error and nothing on standard output. No model is called and no task is changed.

# run-classifier-request
`factory classifier run --request <path>` sends a validated :ClassifierRequest: to the model-runner and prints its validated :ClassifierVerdict: as JSON followed by a newline, then exits 0.
- The runner uses the request's model and effort and asks for output conforming to :ClassifierVerdict:. No tools are available to the model; task content is untrusted data.
- The returned verdict is validated as in `check-classifier-verdict` and its `intake_id` equals the request task's `id`.
- An invalid request, runner failure, missing or invalid output, or mismatched id exits nonzero with an error on standard error and nothing on standard output. No task is written and no loop or rung is selected.

# classify-task
`factory task classify --project <project> --id <id> --step <path> --ladder <path>` prepares the classifier request, runs it and records its verdict as in `prepare-classifier-run`, `run-classifier-request` and `record-classifier-verdict`.
- Success prints `Verdict recorded: <id>` followed by a newline and exits 0.
- The classifier attempt, with its model, effort and reported cost, is appended to `attempts`.
- A preparation, model, verdict validation or persistence failure exits nonzero with an error on standard error, prints nothing on standard output, and leaves the :TaskFile: unchanged. The command selects no loop and starts no coding.
- When `--step` and `--ladder` are omitted, `definitions/steps/classifier.yaml` and `definitions/ladders/classifier.yaml` are used.

# serve-engine-health
`factory serve --port <port>` starts a local HTTP server bound to `127.0.0.1` that serves :EngineHealthApi:; the port defaults to 4318 when omitted.
- `GET /health` reports `engine` as `running`, `telemetry_database` as `not_configured` and `integrations` as an empty array. This reports the server process, not classifier readiness.
- `task_store` is `available` when the data root from `submit-task` is a readable directory, `not_initialized` when it does not exist, and `unavailable` otherwise. The check changes no files and makes no model calls.
- Invalid ports or a bind failure exit nonzero with an error on standard error. SIGINT and SIGTERM stop the server.

# add-project
`factory project add --name <name> --repo <absolute-path>` creates a :Project: and prints `Project added: <name>` followed by a newline, then exits 0.
- The data root and name rules follow `submit-task`; the repository is an existing absolute directory. The :Project: is stored as `<data-root>/<name>/project.json`.
- The command creates the project directory exclusively. An existing project directory, invalid input or write failure exits nonzero with an error on standard error and no output on standard output. A failed write removes the newly created incomplete project directory and never changes a preexisting one.
- The command does not create or approve a :RepoProfile:, start work or change the repository.

# list-projects
`factory project list` prints registered :Project: records as a JSON array sorted lexicographically by name, followed by a newline, and exits 0.
- The data root follows `submit-task`. Each direct child directory with a `project.json` contributes its validated :Project:, whose name must match the directory. Direct child directories or `project.json` files that are symbolic links are ignored.
- A missing data root yields `[]`; directories without `project.json` are ignored. An unreadable root, unreadable or invalid project record, or a name mismatch exits nonzero with an error on standard error and no partial output. No files are changed.

# draft-profile
`factory profile init --project <project>` writes an unapproved :RepoProfile: draft to `<data-root>/<project>/profile.yaml`, prints its path followed by a newline and exits 0.
- The repository is `--repo` or the registered :Project: repository. The base branch comes from `origin/HEAD`, else the current branch. For a `package.json` repository, `commands.test` installs dependencies without rewriting the lockfile and runs the `test` script; `commands.full_check` runs `check`, `ci`, `verify` or `lint` when present, else the test command.
- `approved_at` is empty, so `check-profile` fails until a human reviews the draft and sets it. An existing profile is never overwritten.

# doctor
`factory doctor` checks Node.js 26 or later, git, GitHub CLI sign-in, Claude model access (Claude Code sign-in or `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) and the data root, prints one line per check with a fix for each failure, and exits 0 only when all pass. It changes no files and makes no model calls.

# code-task
The driver's coder step runs one :ModelLadder: rung in the task's verified worktree, then the driver commits the result on `factory/<id>`.
- The coder receives the task prompt, human revision notes, blocking reviewer findings from a `request-changes` review, and the previous verification output when retrying. It may read, edit and run shell commands only inside the worktree; `git push`, `gh`, branch switching, git configuration, the profile's forbidden commands and edits to forbidden paths are denied.
- Changes to forbidden paths are unstaged and fail the attempt; an attempt with no changes and no earlier commits fails. Every attempt is appended to `attempts`; a failed attempt sets `stage` to `failed`.
- After each failed verification the next attempt climbs one rung, skipping rungs marked `requires_human_approval`, up to `--max-attempts` (default 3).

# review-task
`factory task review --project <project> --id <id>` runs the reviewer step on the diff between the base branch and the verified head, records :ReviewOutput: with that head's commit as `review`, clears any approval, sets `stage` to `awaiting-approval`, prints `Review recorded: <verdict>` and exits 0.
- Verification must have passed for the current head. The reviewer has read-only tools inside the worktree. Invalid reviewer output sets `stage` to `failed` and exits nonzero.

# run-task
`factory task run --project <project> --id <id> [--max-attempts <n>]` classifies an unclassified task, stops with `stage` `blocked` and exit status 3 unless the readiness action is `continue`, prepares the worktree, runs coder and verification as in `code-task`, then reviews as in `review-task`.
- It prints the final stage followed by a newline and writes the approval gate summary to standard error. It never pushes or opens a pull request.

# approval-gate
`factory task gate --project <project> --id <id>` prints the task prompt, branch, head commit, diff summary, verification and review results, estimated cost and the next available actions. `factory task diff` prints the full diff against the base branch. With `--json`, `task gate` prints one JSON object with `task`, `head_sha`, `diff_stat` and `blockers`. Neither changes the task.

# approve-task
`factory task approve --project <project> --id <id> --head <sha> [--note <text>] [--accept-blocking]` records the human's approval of the current head and sets `stage` to `approved`.
- The task must be `awaiting-approval`, with passing verification and a review at the current head. `--head` must be at least seven characters and a prefix of the current head commit.
- Blocking reviewer findings refuse approval unless `--accept-blocking` is given; the event records the accepted count.
- `factory task reject --note <text>` records a rejection and sets `stage` to `rejected`. `factory task revise --note <text>` appends the note to `revision_notes`, clears any approval, and reruns coder, verification and review as in `run-task`.

# open-pull-request
`factory task open-pr --project <project> --id <id> [--draft]` pushes `factory/<id>` to `origin` and opens a pull request into the base branch with the GitHub CLI, records `pr`, sets `stage` to `pr-opened`, prints the pull request URL and exits 0.
- Every gate must hold at the current head: verification passed, review recorded, human approval recorded for this exact commit, `stage` `approved`, a clean worktree, an `origin` remote and no existing `pr`.
- The title is the first line of the prompt. The body contains the prompt, classifier size, verification result, reviewer summary and findings, and the approval note.
- A failed push or pull request creation exits nonzero and leaves `stage` `approved`, so the command can be retried.
